{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-9928b087-4641-41fa-bffe-b72b2e6ce1da","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b8d35eef5abcfd66","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9010-vtcsvjs2/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9010-E002.md","content":"# Emit audit events for KServe inference traffic with at-least-once delivery\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic implements audit event emission in the KServe inference path so that every inference request through a KServe endpoint produces a tamper-evident, structured audit record, and delivers those records to the cluster audit sink reliably. It is the core compliance deliverable of the strategy: financial-services customers require a record of who invoked which model endpoint, when, and with what outcome.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Emit one audit event per inference request processed through a KServe InferenceService endpoint, capturing caller identity, model/version, timestamp, and response status — not payloads.\n- Emit events conforming to the published common inference audit event schema.\n- Deliver events to the cluster audit sink (the standard cluster logging forwarder) with at-least-once semantics.\n- Ensure no audit events are lost on component restart: undelivered events must survive restart of the emitting component (transient local buffering for delivery reliability only — long-term storage and retention remain customer-provided and out of scope).\n- Define and read the per-project audit enablement configuration that controls whether inference auditing is active, so the audit capability can be toggled per project by administrators.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given an inference request through a KServe endpoint, when the request completes, then an audit event is emitted containing caller identity, model/version, timestamp, and response status, and no request or response payload content.\n- Given audit events are emitted, when they are inspected at the cluster audit sink, then they conform to the published common audit event schema.\n- Given the audit sink is temporarily unavailable or the emitting component restarts with events not yet delivered, when delivery is re-attempted, then all buffered events are eventually delivered at least once (no loss on component restart).\n- Given inference auditing is disabled for a project, when inference requests are served in that project, then no audit events are emitted for them; given it is enabled, events are emitted.\n- Audit emission does not alter inference request/response behavior beyond adding the audit record (payloads are never captured).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-1 (P0)** — every inference request through a KServe endpoint emits an audit event capturing caller identity, model/version, timestamp, and response status (not payloads).\n- **HLR-2 (P0)** — audit events are delivered to the cluster audit sink with at-least-once semantics and are not lost on component restart.\n- Supports the enablement surface required by HLR-3 (per-project on/off), whose user-facing control is delivered separately by the Dashboard work.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":27849,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b0a662afd2f09df","agent_id":"a0954d66229d27e1c","session_id":"2216ab2c-51dc-48a5-83cb-2b16959bc098","uuid":"d7e5d255-3574-4ce6-9203-89f00c8f896b","timestamp":"2026-10-21T23:58:52.435Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9010"}
