{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8a16ff68aad4489e","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9010-E002\n3\ttitle: Emit audit events for KServe inference traffic with at-least-once delivery\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9010\n5\tcomponent: KServe\n6\tteam: RHOAI Serving\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-9010-E001\n11\tai_signals:\n12\t  change_specificity: 0\n13\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n14\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n15\t  existing_foundation: 1\n16\t  open_questions: 1\n17\t  external_dependency: 1\n18\t  human_process_gates: 1\n19\t  repo_access: 1\n20\t  architecture_claims: 0\n21\timplementation_type: null\n22\tinvestigation_signals: null\n23\tai_implementability: High\n24\tai_implementability_score: 6\n25\tjira_key: null\n26\tbranch: null\n27\tgated_by: null\n28\tgate_failure_impact: null\n29\t---\n30\t# Emit audit events for KServe inference traffic with at-least-once delivery\n31\t\n32\t## Description\n33\t\n34\tThis epic implements audit event emission in the KServe inference path so that every inference request through a KServe endpoint produces a tamper-evident, structured audit record, and delivers those records to the cluster audit sink reliably. It is the core compliance deliverable of the strategy: financial-services customers require a record of who invoked which model endpoint, when, and with what outcome.\n35\t\n36\t## Scope\n37\t\n38\t- Emit one audit event per inference request processed through a KServe InferenceService endpoint, capturing caller identity, model/version, timestamp, and response status — not payloads.\n39\t- Emit events conforming to the published common inference audit event schema.\n40\t- Deliver events to the cluster audit sink (the standard cluster logging forwarder) with at-least-once semantics.\n41\t- Ensure no audit events are lost on component restart: undelivered events must survive restart of the emitting component (transient local buffering for delivery reliability only — long-term storage and retention remain customer-provided and out of scope).\n42\t- Define and read the per-project audit enablement configuration that controls whether inference auditing is active, so the audit capability can be toggled per project by administrators.\n43\t\n44\t## Acceptance Criteria\n45\t\n46\t- Given an inference request through a KServe endpoint, when the request completes, then an audit event is emitted containing caller identity, model/version, timestamp, and response status, and no request or response payload content.\n47\t- Given audit events are emitted, when they are inspected at the cluster audit sink, then they conform to the published common audit event schema.\n48\t- Given the audit sink is temporarily unavailable or the emitting component restarts with events not yet delivered, when delivery is re-attempted, then all buffered events are eventually delivered at least once (no loss on component restart).\n49\t- Given inference auditing is disabled for a project, when inference requests are served in that project, then no audit events are emitted for them; given it is enabled, events are emitted.\n50\t- Audit emission does not alter inference request/response behavior beyond adding the audit record (payloads are never captured).\n51\t\n52\t## HLR Traceability\n53\t\n54\t- **HLR-1 (P0)** — every inference request through a KServe endpoint emits an audit event capturing caller identity, model/version, timestamp, and response status (not payloads).\n55\t- **HLR-2 (P0)** — audit events are delivered to the cluster audit sink with at-least-once semantics and are not lost on component restart.\n56\t- Supports the enablement surface required by HLR-3 (per-project on/off), whose user-facing control is delivered separately by the Dashboard work.\n57\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-94597b3738fec048","agent_id":"a5076f2e00e677d4c","session_id":"2216ab2c-51dc-48a5-83cb-2b16959bc098","uu