{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9dc37d70a6798d07","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9010\n3\tscore: 13\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n9\t  description: E001 ai_signals.human_process_gates=0 contradicts its own rationale\n10\t    (no formal human approval gate specified) and the +1 scored on E002/E003 for the\n11\t    identical condition; per the rubric, no human approval step maps to +1.\n12\terror: null\n13\t---\n14\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9010\n15\t\n16\t## Review Summary\n17\t\n18\tScore: 13/14 — pass\n19\tRecommendation: accept\n20\t\n21\t## Criterion Details\n22\t\n23\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n24\tAll five HLRs are mapped in the traceability matrix and confirmed in epic \"HLR Traceability\" sections: HLR-1 and HLR-2 (P0) → E002, HLR-3 (P1) → E003, HLR-4 (P2) → E001, HLR-5 (P2) → E004. Priority inheritance is correct: E001 is promoted to P0 because the strategy itself states HLR-1 (P0) cannot ship before the schema is settled — the prerequisite epic correctly does not have lower priority than the work it enables. E004's P0 is correctly derived from the strategy's `priority: Critical` via the docs-authoring priority exception, so its dependency on P1 E003 is not a violation (Rule 11 edges do not trigger priority inheritance). No priority collapse: E002 bundles HLR-1 and HLR-2, both P0 and the same logical change (emission and its delivery semantics); no deferrable lower-priority feature is trapped inside a higher-priority epic.\n25\t\n26\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n27\tNo circular dependencies. Every edge in the decomposition summary diagram matches the target epic's frontmatter `dependencies` exactly (E001: [], E002: [E001], E003: [E002], E004: [E001, E002, E003]) and arrows are drawn dependency → dependent per the diagram convention. Edge justification is sound: E001 → E002 is a genuine build-against-artifact dependency (the strategy explicitly states HLR-1 cannot ship before the schema is settled); E002 → E003 is justified by Rule 13 — E002's scope defines the per-project audit enablement configuration surface that the Dashboard toggle builds against (a design contract, not a mere image digest/endpoint configuration reference, so a `dependencies` edge rather than an AC gate is appropriate); the three edges into E004 are valid Rule 11 docs-authoring-last edges. No konflux-onboarding, license-validation, or repo-onboarding epics exist, so the mandated-parallel pairings (rules 7, 8, 10) do not apply — both components are confirmed onboarded via architecture context. Critical path length 4 (E001 → E002 → E003 → E004) is within the M-strategy heuristic (3-4). No conditional branch files are used, so branch metadata checks do not apply. No data/artifact dependency discoverable in epic content is missing from the DAG: E004's interpretation guidance consumes E001's schema (edge present) and E003's toggle (edge present).\n28\t\n29\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n30\tEach epic has a single coherent component/team tuple: E001/E002 (KServe, RHOAI Serving), E003 (Dashboard, RHOAI Dashboard), E004 (Documentation, RHOAI Serving). The E001/E002 split within the same team/component is correctly drawn along work-product lines — a published cross-component schema contract versus serving-path code — and is explicitly justified in the summary. No scope duplication: E002 defines the serving-side enablement configuration while E003 builds the Dashboard control that writes it; these are complementary halves of one surface, not independent builds of the same thing. No epic mixes code implementation with content authoring (E004 is pure docs-authoring). No epic appears to exceed ~2 weeks of work.\n31\t\n32\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n33\tAll four epics are Implementation and each produces a concrete artifact (published schema, emission/delivery code, Dashboard control, documentation). No Investigation epics exist, which is correct: the strategy states open questions are none, the audit sink interface is fixed as the standard cluster logging forwarder, and the remaining unknowns (buffering mechanism, enable/disable propagation mechanism) are implementation choices that do not change which downstream epics exist — the decomposition explicitly reasons through this. All `gated_by` fields are null and no epic lists an Investigation in `dependencies`, so gating metadata checks are vacuously satisfied.\n34\t\n35\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n36\tAll epics are Implementation type and correctly carry the 9-signal `ai_signals` field (no `investigation_signals` misuse); all four ai-signals rationale files exist and their rationales match the frontmatter values. One signal value is inconsistent with its own rationale: E001 scores `human_process_gates: 0` while its rationale states \"no formal human approval gate is specified by the strategy\" — per the rubric, no human approval step maps to +1, and E002/E003 score +1 for the materially identical condition (\"no human approval steps beyond ordinary code review\"). E004's 0 is defensible since its technical-review AC is a genuine human review step. Remaining signals check out against the strategy content: E001's `existing_foundation: -1` (greenfield schema) and `change_specificity: +1` (fields enumerated), E002's `change_specificity: 0` (no hook points or buffering mechanism specified), and `open_questions: +1` across E001/E002/E004 are all consistent with the strategy's \"Open Questions: None\". Per instructions, the computed `ai_implementability` totals were not re-derived.\n37\t\n38\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n39\tEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria derived from the strategy, mostly in Given/When/Then form. Rule-mandated ACs are present where applicable: E004 (docs-authoring) has the technical-review-before-publication AC (Rule 26); no konflux-chain 