{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b55133bc2d4153e0","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9010-E001\n3\ttitle: Define and publish the common inference audit event schema\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9010\n5\tcomponent: KServe\n6\tteam: RHOAI Serving\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 1\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: -1\n15\t  open_questions: 1\n16\t  external_dependency: 1\n17\t  human_process_gates: 0\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 0\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: High\n23\tai_implementability_score: 3\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# Define and publish the common inference audit event schema\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tThis epic defines and publishes the common audit event schema for inference traffic on RHOAI. The schema is the platform-level contract that inference audit events must conform to and that future components (pipelines, workbenches) will adopt in later strategies so their events remain compatible. It is a design-and-publication deliverable, not a code change in the serving path.\n34\t\n35\t## Scope\n36\t\n37\t- Define a structured audit event schema capturing, per inference request: caller identity, model and version, timestamp, and response status.\n38\t- Explicitly exclude request/response payloads from the schema (audit events carry metadata only).\n39\t- Ensure the schema is expressible through the standard cluster logging forwarder, which is the cluster audit sink.\n40\t- Publish the schema in a location and form that downstream components and future strategies can consume and extend.\n41\t\n42\t## Acceptance Criteria\n43\t\n44\t- The schema defines fields for caller identity, model/version, timestamp, and response status, and mandates that no payload content is carried in audit events.\n45\t- The schema is validated against the delivery path of the standard cluster logging forwarder (events structured per the schema can be forwarded to the cluster audit sink).\n46\t- The schema is published and referenced such that a future component (e.g., pipelines, workbenches) could emit compatible events without further negotiation of the format.\n47\t- The strategy's requirement that inference audit events cannot ship before the schema is settled is satisfied: the schema is settled and available before emission work completes.\n48\t\n49\t## HLR Traceability\n50\t\n51\t- **HLR-4 (P2, promoted to P0 via priority inheritance)** — a common audit event schema is defined and published so future components emit compatible events. This epic is the complete delivery of HLR-4 and is the prerequisite for the P0 inference audit emission requirement (HLR-1).\n52\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9ca28245831feb1e","agent_id":"abc59b94f8c7b9bbe","session_id":"2216ab2c-51dc-48a5-83cb-2b16959bc098","uuid":"5db2739a-ae84-4228-9971-5a320f4f7748","timestamp":"2026-10-22T00:04:55.905Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b55133bc2d4153e0","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9010 decomposition"}
