rving-side implementation defines; the Dashboard's code builds against that configuration surface. The concrete mechanism (CR field, config resource) is an implementation decision for the serving team and is not specified here. |\n37\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Documentation interprets events against the published schema; blocked by all Implementation epics in the strategy. |\n38\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Docs describe the emission capability that was built. |\n39\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Docs cover enabling auditing via the Dashboard toggle. |\n40\t\n41\tE001 and E002 are the same team and component but are split by work-product type: E001 produces a published schema artifact (a cross-component contract consumed by future strategies for pipelines and workbenches), while E002 is the code change in the KServe inference path. Merging them would bury a reusable platform contract inside a component implementation.\n42\t\n43\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n44\t\n45\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n46\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n47\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E002 | Event emission in the KServe inference path; conformance to the published schema is an acceptance criterion. |\n48\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E002 | At-least-once delivery to the cluster audit sink and restart-survival are delivery semantics of the same emission pipeline — same logical change, same epic. |\n49\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E003 | Dashboard enable/disable per project. |\n50\t| HLR-4 | P2 → P0 (inherited) | E001 | Schema definition and publication. Labeled P2 in the strategy but gates all P0 work (HLR-1), so the epic inherits P0 by priority inheritance. |\n51\t| HLR-5 | P2 | E004 | Documentation; docs-authoring priority derived from strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical` → P0 per the docs-authoring priority exception. |\n52\t\n53\tAll five HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n54\t\n55\t## Health Warnings\n56\t\n57\t- **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking):** The strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no per-component owner/change description (Affected Components is a bare list), no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements. Teams were taken from architecture context (KServe → RHOAI Serving, Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard).\n58\t- **Priority inversion risk (informational):** HLR-4 is labeled P2 yet the strategy itself states HLR-1 (P0) cannot ship before the schema is settled — the schema epic is promoted to P0 via priority inheritance. The HLR-4 label understates its blocking nature.\n59\t- **Scope trap (informational):** HLR-2's \"not lost on component restart\" implies transient local buffering of undelivered events inside KServe. Long-term storage and retention are explicitly out of scope (customer-provided); the epic must treat buffering as delivery reliability only, not a storage subsystem.\n60\t- **Docs epic ownership unspecified:** The strategy names no documentation owner. RHOAI Serving is assigned as the feature owner; a human should confirm whether a dedicated documentation team takes this epic.\n61\t\n62\t## Component Mapping Notes\n63\t\n64\t- KServe and Dashboard are canonical components in `.context/rhai-components.txt` and both have architecture context files (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`, `dashboard.md`) — no onboarding chain required; both are Konflux-onboarded per architecture context, so no konflux-onboarding epic or \"build pipeline green\" AC (Rule 25) is generated.\n65\t- The docs epic uses the canonical `Documentation` component. Documentation has no architecture context file, but it is a docs work-product epic (`implementation_type: docs-authoring`), not a code component — the new-component onboarding rule does not apply.\n66\t\n67\t## Investigation Epics\n68\t\n69\tNone. The strategy explicitly states open questions are none, and the audit sink interface is identified as the standard cluster logging forwarder. No conditional ADRs, pending reviews, or unknowns that would change which epics exist. The buffering mechanism for at-least-once delivery and the enable/disable propagation mechanism are implementation details (acceptance criteria on E002/E003), not genuine unknowns.\n70\t\n71\t## Tiered Delivery\n72\t\n73\tNot applicable. No external dependency with uncertain timing: the audit sink is the in-cluster standard logging forwarder, and no upstream contributions are required.\n74\t\n75\t## Staff Engineer Input\n76\t\n77\tNo Staff Engineer Input section is present in the strategy; decomposition follows the strategy body.\n78\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9ca28245831feb1e","agent_id":"abc59b94f8c7b9bbe","session_id":"2216ab2c-51dc-48a5-83cb-2b16959bc098","uuid":"434430aa-f3da-4fb6-8647-82ed7184ced6","timestamp":"2026-10-22T00:05:09.085Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a2f6f0378c02abf1","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9010 decomposition"}
