inguishable.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\nEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria, mostly in Given/When/Then form. Rule-mandated ACs: E004 (docs-authoring) includes the technical-review-before-publication AC; no konflux chain exists so Rule 25 does not apply (verified against architecture context); no epic replaces an existing capability, so the rollback/feature-flag rule does not apply — the per-project toggle itself provides the kill switch. E002's ACs cover the enable/disable behavior, payload exclusion, schema conformance, and restart-survival. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID.\n\nMinor: E001's fourth AC (\"the schema is settled and available before emission work completes\") restates the E001→E002 DAG dependency rather than a testable property of the schema deliverable itself.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\nFull strategy scope is covered: emission fields (caller identity, model/version, timestamp, response status, no payloads) flow consistently through E001 (schema), E002 (emission), and E004 (interpretation docs); per-project toggling is split correctly across E002 (serving side) and E003 (Dashboard); at-least-once delivery with restart survival is in E002; the HLR-5 documentation requirement has a dedicated docs epic. Out-of-scope boundaries are respected and restated (non-inference traffic excluded; long-term storage customer-provided). The strategy's \"open questions: none\" context is carried in the summary. No conditional branches are needed (no bounded outcomes in the strategy). Cross-epic consistency holds: every module of the upstream epics' scope is accounted for downstream. A docs-authoring epic is present as HLR-5 requires.\n\nMinor: \"tamper-evident\" from the problem statement appears in E002's description but no acceptance criterion addresses it — presumably it is a property of the cluster audit sink rather than the emission code, and this should be stated explicitly so the claim is not silently untested.\n"}}]